
Player: This Atomon seems to have a different electron arrangement from Neon.

Player: I wonder how many electrons it has in its outermost energy level.

Player: What is the electron arrangement of Sodium?

- 2,8,1
  Player: Correct! Sodium has eleven electrons arranged as 2,8,1.

- 2,8
  Player: Not quite. That is the electron arrangement of Neon. Sodium has one more electron.

- 2,7
  Player: Not quite. Sodium has eleven electrons. Its electron arrangement is 2,8,1.

Player: So Sodium has only one valence electron!

Player: Its outermost energy level is not complete.

Player: Dr. Mundo said that atoms can become more stable by gaining, losing, or sharing electrons.

Player: Sodium can lose its one valence electron.

Player: When it loses that electron, its arrangement becomes 2,8, like Neon.

Player: It then becomes a positively charged ion called Na+.

Player: I understand! Losing one electron can give Sodium a more stable electron arrangement.

[signal arg="quest_notify:quest_chain_002_01:observe_sodium"]

Player: I should compare this with the next Atomon.
